      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "Why the different speeds? In each reaction, every metal atom loses its one outer electron, and becomes a positive ion. The water takes that electron, which is what makes the hydrogen and the hydroxide. Last time, we saw that going down the group, the outer electron is further from the nucleus, with the same core charge, so it's held less tightly. Potassium gives up its electron most readily, so its reaction runs fastest. A faster reaction also heats the metal faster, so sodium and potassium melt, and potassium's hydrogen catches fire.",
