      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "That last condition matters. Alkali metals react with oxygen and water vapour in the air, so a dull film, called tarnish, forms on the surface. That's another reason they're kept under oil. Cut sodium, and the fresh face is shiny silver, but it goes dull within minutes. A thick film separates the metal from the water at first, so the reaction can start more slowly. A slow start doesn't mean a less reactive metal.",
