      "requestAttempts": 1,
      "pronunciationText": "Last time, we saw that losing the outer electron gets easier as you go down Group one. Now let's see what that looks like. Lithium, sodium and potassium are all Group one metals, called the alkali metals. Each atom has one outer electron, and each metal makes the same products with water. But drop them in, and lithium just fizzes calmly, while potassium bursts into flame. So why do Group one metals react so differently with water?",
      "humanListening": "pending"
    },
